## Running it locally

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

Opens at http://localhost:5173

## Setting up the contact form (EmailJS)

The contact form sends messages straight to your inbox with no backend server.

1. Create a free account at https://www.emailjs.com
2. Add an Email Service (connect your Gmail — saadkhawar169@gmail.com)
3. Create an Email Template. It must include these fields matching the form:
   - `from_name`
   - `reply_to`
   - `message`
4. Copy your **Service ID**, **Template ID**, and **Public Key**
5. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill them in:

```
VITE_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=service_xxxxxxx
VITE_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=template_xxxxxxx
VITE_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
```

6. Restart `npm run dev` after adding the `.env` file (Vite only reads env vars on startup).

The `.env` file is gitignored — never commit it. When you deploy to Vercel,
add these same three values under Project Settings → Environment Variables.

## Adding your photo (optional)

Drop a square photo at `public/photo.jpg`, then in `src/pages/About.jsx`
uncomment the `<img>` tag inside the photo slot and remove the "MSK" placeholder span.

## Deploying to Vercel

1. Push this project to a GitHub repository
2. Go to https://vercel.com → New Project → import the repo
3. Vercel auto-detects Vite — no build config needed
4. Add your three `VITE_EMAILJS_*` environment variables under Project Settings
5. Deploy

`vercel.json` is already included so that navigating directly to a URL like
`yoursite.com/projects` (not just clicking through the nav) works correctly
instead of 404ing — this is a common gotcha with client-side routing.

## Editing your content

You will almost never need to touch component code to update text:

- **Projects** → edit `src/data/projects.js`
- **Skills / journey stages** → edit `src/data/skills.js`
- **Contact links** → edit the `channels` array directly in `src/pages/Contact.jsx`
- **Nav links** → edit the `links` array in `src/layout/Navbar.jsx`

## Design notes

- Colors, fonts, and spacing tokens are defined once in `src/index.css` under `@theme`
- The 3D shape on the homepage is procedural (no external `.glb` model needed) —
  this keeps the repo lightweight and avoids asset-loading complexity. If you
  want a custom model later, drop a `.glb` file in `public/models/` and swap
  `FloatingShape.jsx` for a `useGLTF`-based component.
- Lenis (smooth scroll) is initialized once, globally, in `App.jsx` — every
  route inherits it automatically.
